From: Banana <bananalinux@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] runloop: a core's minimum audio latency must not kill the audio driver

RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_MINIMUM_AUDIO_LATENCY reinitialises the audio driver so the
new buffer size takes effect. On the Retroid Pocket Classic's audioreach frontend
the PlayStation cores' unconditional 192 ms request cannot be honoured: the reopen
refuses the buffer, then the periods, then snd_pcm_hw_params(), so the driver comes
back dead and RetroArch runs the whole session with no audio -- PSX silent, while
every core that asks for nothing (GBA, the menu) is fine. Keep the buffer the
driver already opened with (the configured 64 ms) and only remember the request.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/runloop.c
+++ b/runloop.c
@@ -2465,8 +2465,13 @@
 
             RARCH_LOG("[Environ] Setting audio latency to %u ms.\n", audio_latency_new);
 
-            command_event(CMD_EVENT_REINIT, &reinit_flags);
-            video_driver_set_aspect_ratio();
+            /* Banana: never tear the audio driver down for a core's latency wish. On the Retroid Pocket
+             * Classic's audioreach frontend the PlayStation cores' 192 ms request cannot be honoured, and
+             * reopening the device for it (which the audioreach frontend refuses, periods and all) leaves
+             * RetroArch with no audio at all -- the game (and, afterwards, the menu) runs silent. The
+             * driver keeps the buffer it opened with (the configured 64 ms, the shape every working stream
+             * here uses); the core's wish is only remembered. */
+            (void)reinit_flags;
 
             /* Cannot continue recording with different
              * parameters.
